<h2>Repair or Replace? How the Scope Gets Decided</h2>
<p>The biggest dollar question on most roof claims is whether the insurer pays for spot repairs or a full replacement. Factors that often matter include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Extent of damage</strong> on each slope, often recorded with test squares.</li>
<li><strong>Repairability.</strong> Older shingles can become brittle, and lifting them to make a repair can cause more damage.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Discontinued shingles, colors, or metal profiles may not be available.</li>
<li><strong>Code and permit requirements.</strong> Corpus Christi adopted the 2021 International Residential Code with local amendments effective August 1, 2023 (<a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/department-directory/development-services/construction-codes-and-ordinances/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">City of Corpus Christi</a>). The city's homeowner permitting guide lists replacing more than 100 square feet of shingles, removing and replacing decking, and changing roof material among the work that needs a permit. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Code-required upgrades are typically paid only under ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit. Code requirements alone do not create coverage.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Windstorm Certification and TWIA Eligibility</h2>
<p>Corpus Christi and the rest of Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties are in the TDI windstorm catastrophe area. The TDI Windstorm Inspection Program covers re-roofs and roof repairs there, and inspections are done by appointed Texas-licensed engineers or TDI inspectors (<a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/generalquestio.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI</a>). Starting April 1, 2026, WPI-1 applications must be certified to the 2024 IRC or IBC. Uncertified work can affect eligibility for TWIA coverage, which many coastal owners rely on for wind and hail. Our <a href="/windstorm-certificate-wpi-8-insurance-claims/">WPI-8 windstorm certificate guide</a> explains more.</p>

<h2>Reviewing the Insurer's Roof Estimate</h2>
<p>A roof estimate can look complete and still leave out real costs. Items that are often missing or underpriced include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tear-off and disposal of each existing layer.</li>
<li>Drip edge, starter course, hip and ridge caps, and valley materials.</li>
<li>Underlayment and ice-and-water or self-adhered membranes where required.</li>
<li>Flashing, pipe jacks, vents, and roof-mounted equipment detach and reset.</li>
<li>Decking replacement where boards are damaged or deteriorated.</li>
<li>Steep-roof and high-roof charges, and overhead and profit where a general contractor is reasonably needed.</li>
<li>Windstorm engineering or inspection costs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a> covers this in depth. If items were missed after payment, an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> may be appropriate.</p>

<h2>Depreciation and Roof Payment Schedules</h2>
<p>Under replacement cost coverage, the insurer typically pays actual cash value first, which is replacement cost minus depreciation, and releases recoverable depreciation after the roof is replaced, within the deadline in the policy. Some policies limit roof coverage to actual cash value or use a payment schedule based on roof age, which can sharply reduce what an older roof receives. Read your declarations page and endorsements before you sign a roofing contract, and use our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> to see how deductibles and depreciation affect the check.</p>

<h2>Interior Damage From a Damaged Roof</h2>
<p>A roof claim is often more than a roof claim. When hail punctures a membrane or wind tears off shingles, rain can soak attic insulation, stain ceilings, and run down inside walls before anyone notices. Many policies cover that interior water damage when the storm created the opening, subject to policy terms. Document wet ceilings, drywall, insulation, flooring, and contents along with the roof itself, and dry things out quickly so the loss does not grow into a mold problem. Delayed drying can give an insurer reason to argue that some of the damage came from neglect rather than the storm. If water has already spread, our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page explains what to expect on that side of the claim.</p>
<p>Salt air is another local factor. Fasteners, flashing, and metal roof components closer to the bays and the Gulf can corrode over time, and insurers sometimes point to corrosion as the cause of a failure. Clear photos of fresh storm damage, taken soon after the event, help separate new damage from long-term wear.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the insured only. On a roof claim, that may include a detailed roof inspection with photos and measurements, review of the policy and roof endorsements, a line-item estimate that accounts for code and windstorm requirements, and communication with the insurer's adjuster. Texas law requires a license to act as a public adjuster, and every public adjuster contract must state "WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY" and allow you to rescind in writing within 72 hours of signing. We cannot promise any result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Before You Start Roof Repairs</h2>
<ol>
<li>Photograph the roof and interior damage, and keep damaged samples when practical.</li>
<li>Tarp openings to prevent more water damage, and keep the receipts.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly to the right insurer: your homeowners carrier or TWIA, depending on your coverage.</li>
<li>Let the insurer inspect before permanent repairs, unless waiting would cause more harm.</li>
<li>Confirm permits and windstorm inspection with your roofer before work begins.</li>
</ol>
<p>For questions about deadlines to sue or a dispute that may need legal action, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
